WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.634
-Welcome back to "Late Night,"
everybody.

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:05.367
Lately there has been an
important conversation happening

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:08.667
surrounding the role of nepotism
in the entertainment industry.

00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:10.567
Well, this past week,
Twitter discovered

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:13.434
that one of my writers
is a nepo baby.

00:00:13.434 --> 00:00:16.100
And I want to give him
the opportunity

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.868
to address the controversy,
so here he is -- Ben Warheit.

00:00:18.868 --> 00:00:20.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.801 --> 00:00:23.133
-Hi. Hi.

00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:26.100
Uh, so, I want to address

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.501
what people have been saying
about me.

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:29.767
First of all, I was never trying
to hide who my parents are.

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:32.400
I fully acknowledge
that my upbringing was

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.033
largely responsible
for my career success.

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:37.133
My father is David Warheit.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:39.567
And my mother is
Christine Chastain Warheit.

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:43.667
-Wait. I'm sorry.
How are your parents famous?

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.868
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:00:45.868 --> 00:00:48.834
So, my mom was
the head librarian

00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:51.400
at Christiana hospital
in Delaware.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.567
And my dad was
a research scientist at DuPont

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:57.200
for 30 years.
So, yes, in many respects,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.133
I started on third base
because of my famous parents.

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:03.234
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.234 --> 00:01:06.801
-Sorry. So, your dad was
some kind of scientist?

00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:10.567
-Uh, [Chuckles] yeah, well,
he's one of the top 50 or 60

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.868
fiber toxicologists
on the East Coast.

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:14.300
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.267
I mean, he' no Rick von Susani,
but he's up there.

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.300
[ Laughter ]
He knows him.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.000
-I...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.100
I've never heard of him.
-Alright, well, you know.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.701
You know, that's the thing about
nepotism, though, Seth.

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:29.934
It's not just
about name recognition.

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:32.167
There's a lot of other,
more subtle privileges

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.000
that come with
having famous parents.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.934
-Okay, so,
what sort of privileges

00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:38.834
did you get from them?
-Okay, well, for example,

00:01:38.834 --> 00:01:41.234
when I was growing up,
sometimes my dad

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.200
would go to science conferences
in Europe.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.667
And when he came home,
he would always bring me back

00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:48.367
a Toblerone candy bar.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:52.133
Now, you couldn't get
one of those in Delaware.

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:55.234
[ Laughter ]
Not in Wilmington, not then.

00:01:55.234 --> 00:01:58.133
Now, is that fair
to the other kids?

00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:01.701
No, but it happened.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:04.100
And I'd be lying if I said
it didn't influence

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.901
how I got where I am today.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:09.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.567
-Okay, and your mom,
she does what, again?

00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:14.100
-She was the head librarian
at Christiana Hospital.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.834
-So she was a librarian?
-Yeah, well, she was

00:02:17.834 --> 00:02:21.133
the director of all libraries
at Delaware's biggest hospital.

00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:22.968
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:25.434
So, because she was
so important to doctors,

00:02:25.434 --> 00:02:27.734
you benefited from getting
good medical care,

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.601
letters of recommendation,
stuff like that?

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:32.100
-Yeah. No. Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.400
But every time
I went with her to work,

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.734
I got to eat for free
in the hospital cafeteria,

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:40.234
and I could get
any soup I wanted

00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:41.901
and bring it back to the library
and eat it.

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:43.667
Any soup they had.

00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:45.434
Name, like, a soup. Any soup.

00:02:45.434 --> 00:02:46.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:48.601
-Now? You want me to name --
-Think of any soup.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.634
-Split pea soup.
-If they had it.

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:52.133
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:54.701
You know?
Any soup that they had.

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:56.501
-Mm-hmm.
-So, yeah.

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:58.667
I realized that hearing this...

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:00.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.901 --> 00:03:02.834
...it might make it harder
for people to root for me

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:04.901
and think of me as an Everyman.

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:06.701
But I just want to own up to it.

00:03:06.701 --> 00:03:09.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.901 --> 00:03:13.067
-Ben, I don't think
you're a nepo baby.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:15.467
-I am. It's okay.
-No.

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:18.067
[ Laughter ]
I think you just want your name

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:20.801
associated with
the nepo baby conversation

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:22.501
so you seem more important.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:24.834
-[ Chuckles ]
Well, that's kind, Seth.

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:28.601
You're really being kind, but
I'm fully willing to accept,

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:31.467
you know, all responsibility
for my privileged position.

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:35.100
-Ben, the nepo baby conversation
is about people who benefited

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.767
from having famous people.
And frankly,

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.434
I just don't think
your parents are famous.

00:03:39.434 --> 00:03:42.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:44.667
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.901
It just sounds like
they're regular people.

00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:49.567
-Regular people? Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:52.834
Just run-of-the-mill, ordinary,
"nothing special about them"

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:54.467
kind of people?

00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:58.234
-I mean, not to be rude,
but yeah, sort of.

00:03:58.234 --> 00:04:00.634
[ Laughter ]
-Okay. Okay.

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:02.534
That's interesting.
Maybe you would like

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:04.601
to tell them that
to their faces?

00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:06.334
-What?

00:04:06.334 --> 00:04:08.000
-Oh, my parents are here
at the show.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Hey, Mom and Dad.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:15.267 --> 00:04:17.300
Yeah, hi.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.033
Yeah, Seth --
Seth, wants to tell you

00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:23.467
exactly what he thinks of you.
-No, I -- No, I don't.

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:26.133
-No, no, go on, tell my parents
you don't think they're special.

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:29.067
-Wait a minute!
-Yeah, no, that they're --

00:04:29.067 --> 00:04:30.501
how did you put it,
"a dime a dozen"?

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:33.434
-I never said "a dime a dozen."
-Sure, no, no.

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:36.601
You just implied they're
a couple corn-fed simpletons

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:39.701
of poor breeding stock.
-I would not say that!

00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:42.200
-Yeah, okay, go ahead, Seth,
tell my parents you think

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.667
they're as common as dog [Bleep]
on the sidewalk.

00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:47.667
-Is that what you think
about us, Seth,

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:51.133
that we're as common as
dog [Bleep] on the sidewalk?

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.434
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.434 --> 00:04:55.000
-I don't think that.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.968
-Well, why don't you
just let it out, Seth.

00:04:56.968 --> 00:04:59.267
Tell my dad you think
he's a bad scientist

00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:02.634
that nobody cares about.
-I'm not a bad scientist.

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:05.701
I know Rick von Susani.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.734 --> 00:05:12.868
-I don't --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:12.868 --> 00:05:15.400
I don't think
you're a bad scientist.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.133
-Well, no.
You know what?

00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:19.067
Then this is really messy, Seth,
'cause I'm having

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:21.067
a little bit of trouble
understanding

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:24.133
what exactly it is you do think.
-I think --

00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:25.968
-No, no, no, yeah,
just tell my parents.

00:05:25.968 --> 00:05:27.868
Look them right in the face

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:30.033
and tell them
what you think of them.

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:33.534
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:39.067
-I think, they're famous.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:41.734
-How famous?

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:44.634
-Really, really famous.
-Wow. Wow.

00:05:44.634 --> 00:05:47.601
Hmm, so you probably
want their autograph.

00:05:47.601 --> 00:05:51.534
-I do.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.534 --> 00:05:54.701
I do want their autograph.
-Yeah, yeah, I figured.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:56.501
You know...

00:05:56.501 --> 00:06:01.701
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:01.701 --> 00:06:06.534
These are, um -- These are
gonna be 50 bucks each for you.

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:08.667
And you know why
they're so expensive?

00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:11.334
-Because they're very famous,
very special people.

00:06:11.334 --> 00:06:16.868
-And what are the children of
very special, famous people

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:19.334
called?
-Nepo babies.

00:06:19.334 --> 00:06:20.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.534 --> 00:06:23.100
-What was that?
-Nepo babies.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.234
-A little louder
for the people at home?

00:06:25.234 --> 00:06:27.801
-Nepo babies!

00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:31.067
-Okay, so that would make me...
-A nepo baby.

00:06:31.067 --> 00:06:33.868
-Okay, was that so hard?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:36.000
-I think we're
about out of time.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.234
-Yeah, I'd say so.
-Give a round of applause

00:06:38.234 --> 00:06:41.634
to Ben Warheit and
his very famous people --

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.400
famous parents, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.133
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night.

